diagnosis_text:
A thorough inspection of all topology layers reveals no active fault. The OSPF virtual link between r4 and r8 — the specific component the monitoring ticket describes — is fully operational: both endpoints show a Full OSPF adjacency on VLINK0 (r4 sees 18.1.4.1/Full, r8 sees 14.1.4.1/Full), and area 0.0.0.2 routes (18.1.x.x/32 loopbacks and 80.0.0.0/8) are correctly installed in r1's routing table via the r3→r4→VLINK0→r8 path. The OSPF helper flagged r8's area 0.0.0.2 as "orphaned" because it has zero OSPF adjacencies across its five active interfaces, but this is the intended stub-ABR design: all five interfaces are loopbacks or the single-device BB stub segment, where adjacencies are architecturally impossible. The helper flag is a false positive. The EIGRP/OSPF redistribution domain is also healthy: eigrpd runs on r11, r12, and r13; r11 has Full EIGRP neighbors on both its 112.0.0.0/8 (r12) and 103.0.0.0/8 (r13) segments; and r13's four loopback prefixes (113.1.x.0/24) are redistributed into OSPF at r11 and visible on r1. The "far part went missing after a restart" describes a transient OSPF reconvergence window: when a router restarted, the virtual link dropped and required roughly one dead-interval (~40 s) to re-establish. No flap script, no misconfigured interface, no wrong area assignment, and no daemon outage that persists today. The current state is fully converged and healthy; no corrective action is required.
=== END ===